WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:06.600
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.260
My name is Liz
Roten and today I'm

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.090
going to talk to
you about how you

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.730
can take the project
that you really hate

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:18.690
and turn it into something
oddly satisfying.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.380
So this adventure starts
with a truck, or rather

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.870
a truck corridor study.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.950
Hey, there we go.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:29.990
My manager pitched
this project to me

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.550
as a straightforward,
quick data science win.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.760
The only thing we needed
to do was update the data.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.570
All of the methods and
extra analysis were settled.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.810
It was going to be very easy.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.510
And it wasn't.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.060
And my first inkling
of this was when

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.550
I started to look into
the project materials.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.760
They were bad.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.640
They were pretty bad.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.360
And how bad are we talking here?

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.450
So on the left, you can
see a bunch of files

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.360
and the font is way too small
because there are simply

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.070
too many.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.770
And there are a couple of
things I want to point out.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.940
So there are multiple
final reports.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.820
And the problem, when
you have something

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.340
and you name it and you
have multiple things

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.600
and you name all of them final.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.900
The word final stops
meaning anything.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:33.140
There's no telling what actually
truly is the final document.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.060
There are also
multiple documents

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.250
that were marked as revised.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.150
Great, marked as
revised! And marked

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.330
revised maybe August
11th or August 14th.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.460
However, when this
project first took place,

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.160
it took place over
more than one year.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.020
So was it August 2017 or 2018?

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.180
I don't have any
information from that,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.580
and I don't know who modified it
or revised it, when they revised

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.630
it, or why it was revised.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.820
And there is a single Excel
workbook with all the analysis,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:16.400
including a worksheet with over
15,000 rows and a whole bunch

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.630
of nasty formulas that
will make your skin crawl.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.390
And so now that I've
introduced you to this mess,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.150
I'd like to pivot to
something a little better.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.740
And it's this
internet phenomenon

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:33.330
of things that are
oddly satisfying.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:40.140
So oddly satisfying things is a
content of--a genre of content,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.960
and it's generally
something like someone

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.010
taking something super dirty
and making it pristinely clean

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:52.080
or painting something
very precisely.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.590
I, in my opinion,
find this tiny crab

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.980
holding up a tiny
fish very satisfying

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.245
with the composition.

00:03:00.245 --> 00:03:02.370
You have the yellow and
the blue and the foreground

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.660
and the background.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.110
I think it's satisfying, but in
case that doesn't really do it

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.600
for you, I have a couple more.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.610
So this first video is of
a person with a smoothie.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.270
They add this purple
powder into the smoothie

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.990
and you see the color
come in to the smoothie

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.160
as it's like smoothing around.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.940
And it's very
mesmerizing and lovely.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.740
And I have one more.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.430
And that's of a person using.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.770
A turntable to ice a tart.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.170
So you see the
little tart, you see

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.850
the turntable
turning and a person

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:48.010
using a piping bag to
pipe this perfect spiral.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.650
And then once the
spiral is complete,

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:56.410
they grab a blowtorch, get
a little color on there,

00:03:56.410 --> 00:04:00.610
and finally they pan over to
all of the other absolutely

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.820
beautiful identical tarts.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.790
And there's something
about these videos

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.660
that just soothes my soul.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.700
And so I had this moment of
"What if I take the truck study

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.329
and make it this satisfying?"

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:23.480
I essentially decided
to take my truck

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.260
that made me very
sad and turn it

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:31.310
into the best truck
that has ever existed.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.260
And I learned a few
things along the way

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.800
that I'd like to share with you.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.080
And they come in 3.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.720
First, assess the damage.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.490
Second, do and document.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.775
And finally, leave a gift.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.730
So, let's assess the damage.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.330
You're essentially going to
complete a project intake.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.900
Start by reading
through everything

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.020
and I mean actually everything.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.480
The more you read through
now, the fewer surprises

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.250
you're going to have later
on and the better feel

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.700
you're going to have
for the project itself.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.680
As you're doing
this, you're going

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.820
to make a hate list,
which is a list of all

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.000
of the things about this
project that you hate.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.220
Maybe the
documentation is vague,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.210
maybe the documentation
and the calculation

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:35.200
aren't doing the same thing
that they should be doing.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.090
Whatever it is, write it down
and put it aside for later.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.580
You're also going to
replicate your findings

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:47.840
or the results from the
previous study, if applicable.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.920
And this is going to test
your knowledge of this project,

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.530
and it's going to give
you an opportunity

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.660
to work through it hands-on.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.680
And even if the
numbers don't align

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.940
exactly, you need to be able to
explain why they don't align

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.140
exactly. Was it a rounding
issue, or a data source issue?

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.170
Whatever it is, you
just need to explain it.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.440
And the most important
part of your intake

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:19.510
is finding your thing.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:23.860
And your thing is this
one aspect, or multiple,

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.340
of your professional life
that makes you excited.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:32.950
Something that you know you can
work on and you can be proud of,

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:36.220
and you're going to
find a way to apply

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:39.490
your thing to this project.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.960
Maybe you're really into plot.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.580
You're going to go out and find
all of the ggplot2 extensions

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.115
and make beautiful plots.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.310
Or perhaps you're into maps.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:59.920
You're going to make some maps
with some excellent legend

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.810
legend work, and it'll
all look wonderful.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.050
Or maybe you're into
speed and efficiency.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.960
Break out all of your vector-
based programming skills

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.960
and make this thing
lightning fast.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:17.950
Whatever it is, it's
important that it's yours.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.470
And it's important
it's important that you

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.370
get excited about doing it.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.120
Your thing is what's going
to power this project,

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.980
and it will also keep you sane.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:37.180
And my thing is documentation.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.310
If you're ever unsure
of what your thing could

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:44.320
be with a project,
try documentation.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:50.260
And that leads us to the
second part to do and document.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.740
So you're going to do the
thing, complete the work,

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:57.430
and you're going to
document along the way.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:01.030
There is one sentence
that I think encapsulates

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.250
why documentation is
so important, better

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:08.500
than anything else, and that is
this quote that "Documentation

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:13.030
is a love letter to
your future self."

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:14.920
And this is the
attitude you want

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.710
to go into documentation with.

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.560
And there are folks
across this conference

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.010
that will preach to you the
gospel of documentation.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.790
But there are a couple of
things I would like to add in.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:31.510
One of them is to modularize,
which we did hear about a bit

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.774
this morning in the keynote.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:41.260
So, like this
beautiful tart,

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.570
every slice can be
divided up equally

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:46.810
and stand on its own.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:49.360
And you want to treat
your code like this.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.350
Generally, how do you
know if you need to

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:52.900
modularize your code.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:57.400
If you have a single script with
more than 500 lines, too long,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.410
you should consider modularization.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.720
If you're doing more
than two scrolls,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.450
it's not going to
be sustainable.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.120
So on the left
there is some code

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.860
that does some
spatial processing

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:12.560
and will take a while to run.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.920
It also depends on loading
in a bunch of data sets

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.890
and having libraries
in your environment.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.270
And that's fine.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:20.930
It'll run.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.740
And the script is going
to take a long time.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.340
But if you
modularized, you can go

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:32.890
into a few different, you can
create scripts that are named

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.080
according to what they do.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:40.120
So load packages does
nothing but load packages

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.790
for a solid 15 lines.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.350
Load data only loads the data.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.880
It doesn't do any extra
modeling or spatial processing.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.630
It just loads the data.
And they don't have

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:54.230
to be completely independent.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:55.720
If they need to
run sequentially,

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:59.410
you can name them
1, 2, 3 and that

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.810
gives you a signal that they
need to be run sequentially.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:07.950
I'd also like to advocate for a
bespoke README or a very fancy

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.030
README. A README is an
explanatory document that

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.820
accompanies computer files
or software, according

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.000
to Wikipedia, and
I think it gets

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.640
overlooked in data science,

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:22.950
from my observation.
There are three parts

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.710
of a README that
make it truly bespoke

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:33.800
and fancy: context, structure,
and key information.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.300
So your context should
answer the questions:

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.530
What even is this thing?

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:44.660
Why does this thing exist
and what is its history?

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:47.300
Answer these questions
in the beginning of your

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.720
README and it will ground
this project in what

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.360
the truth and the
true meaning of why

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:54.565
you're even working on this.

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.270
The second is structure.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.330
How is everything organized?

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:03.750
Where does the data live?

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.170
Where do you actually do the
thing to run the analysis

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:10.260
and get the output?

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.890
And this will also force you to
take a step back from your code

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:17.900
and think about it in a
more structural manner.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:20.330
And finally, key information.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:22.460
This is where you
break out the hate list

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:25.540
and you're going to do the
exact opposite of the hate list.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.540
You're going to turn
it into a love list.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.430
And your key information
should answer

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:35.480
the question of at the
most fundamental level,

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.440
what is the operative
component and how is it done?

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.610
So with the truck
corridor study, it

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:47.210
it was a weighted
mean, which wasn't

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:49.520
apparent from the beginning.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.920
And so instead of
leaving it a mystery,

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:56.810
I added it in a snippet of code
that doesn't by itself run,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:02.810
but it gives what is the
most important information.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:06.650
And that's my next
and final part,

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:11.250
is to leave a gift,
not a mystery.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:12.710
I have one more video for you.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:26.250
This is of a person--loading--
a person using a,

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:30.990
gift-wrapping a gift with very
precise, clean lines,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:34.980
and they add a little
bit of nature at the end,

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.830
and it is presented
on a linen tablecloth

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.150
with candles and pine cones.

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:45.390
And I would be utterly delighted
to find a gift like that.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:47.640
And this is the
energy you want to bring

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:50.773
in to your project debrief.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.950
The first thing to do
is to take a break.

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.910
Once the project is done,
really, truly, actually done,

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:02.920
shut it,

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:07.120
put it away, and don't look at it
for at least two, three weeks,

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:08.590
and then you're
going to come back

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.355
in and condense and clarify.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.570
So as I was working
through the truck corridor study,

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:23.150
I made something like nine
R Markdowns and most of them

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:25.880
did not go anywhere.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:29.270
Either they were a dead end,
or some exploratory analysis,

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:34.190
or they were worked into some
other part of the analysis.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:38.090
But you wouldn't know that just
from looking at these files.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:40.970
And if you were going
to work on this project,

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:44.830
you would have to read
through all of these.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:50.770
So instead I broke it down
into three R Markdowns.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:56.140
A README--a bespoke README,--
a summary, and methods,

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:00.140
and it's much more
clear and concise.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:02.440
And we have a
couple aspects that

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.160
are inside those markdowns.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:07.970
In the markdowns,

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:12.820
I've documented not
only what, but why.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:14.590
So, what?

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:21.530
We used 2019 traffic data.
That could be a full sentence.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.460
If I'm looking at
this in the future.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:26.720
And I see that this project
was taking place in 2021,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.840
and they'd already released
the 2020 data, why in the world

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.300
would they use 2019?

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:35.350
And it's likely obvious
to most of us that

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:45.850
2020 was quite anomalous, which
is why we use the 2020 data.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:49.065
It was quite anomalous, also
known as a garbage fire.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:58.640
Additionally, we set--all of
the corridors were given scores

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.310
and those scores were
broken into tiers,

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:06.670
and we set the tier breaks
at 15.4 and 26.2 points.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:09.630
It was a very specific,
and it was important.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:16.560
It's not 15.5, it's
15.4 and not 26.2 or 30,

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:20.880
it's exactly 26.2.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.620
So why would we do that?

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:26.660
Because our
transportation planners

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.930
used their best judgment,
given a wide array of factors

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.425
outside of just these numbers.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:40.050
I also really advocate
for reproducibility.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:41.802
You want to record
package versions,

00:15:41.802 --> 00:15:43.260
and there are lots
of ways that you

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:50.130
can do this, including tools
like groundhog, pak, or renv,

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:50.890
take your pick.

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:52.770
Just be consistent.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:56.290
I'm also a big fan of a
good timestamped output.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:59.310
You can integrate a time stamp
into the name of the file

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:02.250
that you're outputting,
or you can implement it

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:05.460
in a column in a dataframe.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:11.670
Whatever you do, you need to
have a year, month, and day.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:12.425
All three.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:18.770
So this project is
done for me for now.

00:16:18.770 --> 00:16:21.350
And I would love to
tell you about how

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:25.340
I came to love trucks and see
their importance in the Twin

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:30.320
Cities region and how they are
perfect for our future economic

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:31.200
opportunities.

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:35.480
But I still don't
care about trucks.

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:38.000
They're
just not my thing.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:41.810
But every single
person in my office

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:45.590
knows about the documentation
I made for this project.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:49.580
And I am so proud of the
work I did about something

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.070
I don't care about.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.280
And so at the end of it,
I'm feeling less like,

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:56.990
Oh, I don't care about this.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.300
I feel a lot like
this little crab.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:03.800
But instead of a fish,
I'm holding up a truck.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.619
Thank you.

